Checking the PDF the MuPDF renderer writes.

    src/Plugins/MuPDF/tests/pdf-compare.sh [document.tm ...]

from the top of the source tree; with no argument it takes three documents
of the manual. It exports each one through the MuPDF renderer
(TEXMACS_PDF_MUPDF=1) and through whatever the build does otherwise, and
checks the first:

  gs      Ghostscript reads the file without an error and without
          substituting a font. This is the check which earns its keep: an
          invalid font program is the mistake which is easiest to make and
          hardest to see, since the page still looks like a page, only in
          the wrong typeface.
  text    the text extracts, and no character comes out as U+FFFD (a wrong
          encoding, a missing glyph name, a stale ToUnicode).
  render  the page Ghostscript draws and the page MuPDF draws agree to
          within a threshold. They are two independent readers of the same
          file, so a disagreement is a fault in the file -- but note that
          it does *not* catch a substituted font, since both readers then
          substitute; that is what the gs check is for.
  size    reported, not judged. The two routes do not embed the same kind
          of font -- the other one goes through PostScript and comes back
          with bitmaps -- so the numbers are not comparable.

The checks were themselves checked: embedding the Type 1 fonts the wrong
way again (pdf_add_cid_font instead of pdf_add_simple_font) makes the gs
and text checks fail, as it should.

Needs gs, mutool, and python3 with PIL. OUT, WAIT and AGREE override the
output directory, how long a conversion is given, and the threshold.
